>> title: Chinese Chicken Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: chicken, cabbage, sesame, wonton, salad
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: chinese chicken salad, oriental chicken salad, asian chicken salad, chopped chicken salad, brentwood chinese chicken, crispy wonton salad, sesame chicken salad
>> pairs-with: goma-dare, miso-ginger-dressing
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> slack: narrow — the wonton strips are crisp for about twenty minutes after the dressing hits them and are wet cardboard by the hour
>> step.1: poach 15 min, cool in the liquid
>> step.2: fry 2 min, drain hard
>> step.3: shred by hand
>> step.4: slice fine
>> step.5: toss

Poach @chicken breasts{1%lb}(450 g) with @ginger{3%slices}, @scallion{1}(halved) and @kosher salt{1%tsp} in a #saucepan{} of barely trembling water for ~poach{15%min}, then take the pan off the heat and ~cool{20%min} the breasts in their own liquid. Cooling them in the poaching water is the difference between shreddable chicken and dry chicken: pulled out hot, a breast keeps cooking and squeezes its water out onto the board.

Fry @wonton wrappers{8}(stacked and cut in 1/4-in ribbons) in @neutral oil{1%cup}(240 mL) in a #skillet{} for ~fry{2%min}, a handful at a time, and drain them on paper. They go from pale to gold to brown in about fifteen seconds at the end, so pull them one shade lighter than you want them.

Shred @&(~2)poached chicken{} along the grain, by hand, into pieces about the width of a pencil. Two forks make shorter, squarer pieces; hands follow the muscle and give you the long strands that tangle into the cabbage instead of falling through it.

Slice @napa cabbage{4%cups}(shredded fine), @romaine{2%cups}(shredded fine), @carrot{1%large}(julienned), @scallions{3}(on a long bias) and @cilantro{1/2%cup}(leaves and thin stems).

Toss @&(~1)shredded vegetables{} with @&(~2)chicken{}, @goma dare{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @toasted almonds{1/3%cup}(sliced) and @toasted sesame seeds{1%Tbs}, then throw @&(~3)fried wontons{} over the top.

The dressing is the sesame one from the drawer and it wants to be looser here than it is on noodles — a spoonful of water into it before it goes in. Wontons on top and never tossed through: everything under them is wet, and the only reason to fry them was crunch.
